LMAOOO welcome to one of Java's nicest features. 😂 `printf()` is incredibly useful once you know the common format specifiers. You don't need to memorize all of them—just the ones you'll use 95% of the time.

# Most Common Java `printf()` Format Specifiers

| Specifier | Meaning                       | Example            |
| --------- | ----------------------------- | ------------------ |
| `%d`      | Integer                       | `25`               |
| `%f`      | Floating-point (double/float) | `3.141593`         |
| `%s`      | String                        | `"Axel"`           |
| `%c`      | Character                     | `'A'`              |
| `%b`      | Boolean                       | `true`             |
| `%n`      | New line                      | Moves to next line |
| `%%`      | Prints a `%` sign             | `%`                |

---

# Floating Point Formatting

Default:

```java
System.out.printf("%f", 3.14159265);
```

Output

```
3.141593
```

---

2 decimal places

```java
System.out.printf("%.2f", 3.14159265);
```

Output

```
3.14
```

---

3 decimal places

```java
System.out.printf("%.3f", 3.14159265);
```

Output

```
3.142
```

---

No decimal places

```java
System.out.printf("%.0f", 3.14159265);
```

Output

```
3
```

---

# Width Formatting

Minimum width of 10

```java
System.out.printf("%10d", 15);
```

Output

```
        15
```

Notice the spaces.

---

Left aligned

```java
System.out.printf("%-10d", 15);
```

Output

```
15
```

---

Zero padding

```java
System.out.printf("%05d", 15);
```

Output

```
00015
```

---

# Combining Width and Precision

```java
System.out.printf("%8.2f", 12.3456);
```

Output

```
   12.35
```

Meaning

* Width = 8
* Decimal places = 2

---

# Multiple Variables

```java
String name = "Axel";
int age = 20;
double gpa = 1.75;

System.out.printf("%s is %d years old with a GPA of %.2f%n",
        name, age, gpa);
```

Output

```
Axel is 20 years old with a GPA of 1.75
```

---

# Printing a Percent Sign

```java
System.out.printf("Battery: %d%%%n", 95);
```

Output

```
Battery: 95%
```

Notice

```
%%
```

prints a single `%`.

---

# Nice Table Formatting

```java
System.out.printf("%-15s %5d%n", "Math", 98);
System.out.printf("%-15s %5d%n", "Science", 100);
System.out.printf("%-15s %5d%n", "English", 91);
```

Output

```
Math               98
Science           100
English            91
```

This is used **all the time** for CLI applications.

---

# Currency

```java
double price = 299.99;

System.out.printf("$%.2f%n", price);
```

Output

```
$299.99
```

---

# Common Interview/Real Project Example

```java
String student = "John";
double average = 89.45678;

System.out.printf("Student: %-10s Average: %.2f%n",
        student, average);
```

Output

```
Student: John       Average: 89.46
```

